Done. The change is threaded through the existing files as follows:

- [app/config.py](/Users/eranshir/Documents/Projects/deepseek/bench/work/deepseek_t2_multifile_t1/app/config.py) — unchanged; still defines `DEFAULTS` and `Config`.
- [app/loader.py](/Users/eranshir/Documents/Projects/deepseek/bench/work/deepseek_t2_multifile_t1/app/loader.py) — `load_config()` now builds values in the required order: `DEFAULTS`, then `APP_<KEY_UPPERCASE>` env vars (coerced to the default's type), then the explicit `overrides` dict. Unknown `APP_*` vars are ignored since only keys in `DEFAULTS` are looked up.
- [app/main.py](/Users/eranshir/Documents/Projects/deepseek/bench/work/deepseek_t2_multifile_t1/app/main.py) — unchanged; `describe()` picks up the env-driven values through `load_config()`.

Verification results: `APP_PORT=9000 APP_DEBUG=true` prints `localhost:9000 debug=True`; explicit overrides beat env vars (`{'port': 1234, 'debug': False}`); an unknown `APP_UNKNOWN` var is ignored. Bool coercion handles `1/true/True/yes` as true and `0/false/False/no` as false, while a malformed int like `APP_PORT=abc` raises a clear `ValueError` rather than silently misbehaving.
